{
  "title": "The Grid and the Green: Jackson Heights' Streets and Open Space",
  "subtitle": "How a diagonal boulevard, a rectilinear grid, and a chain of private gardens shaped one Queens neighborhood",
  "byline": "Author One; Author Two",
  "template": "docked-sidecar",
  "sources": [
    {"key": "nyc-lpc", "text": "NYC Landmarks Preservation Commission, Jackson Heights Historic District Designation Report"},
    {"key": "city-lore", "text": "City Lore"},
    {"key": "nyc-opendata", "text": "NYC Open Data, Building Footprints and Street Centerline"},
    {"key": "nyc-parks", "text": "NYC Parks Properties"}
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "kind": "street_orientation",
      "id": "claim-northern-diagonal",
      "text": "Northern Boulevard cuts across Jackson Heights at an angle, ignoring the neighborhood's numbered grid.",
      "street": "NORTHERN BLVD",
      "relation": "diagonal_to_grid"
    },
    {
      "kind": "street_orientation",
      "id": "claim-broadway-diagonal",
      "text": "Broadway slices diagonally through the grid as a legacy colonial-era road, predating the planned street system.",
      "street": "BROADWAY",
      "relation": "diagonal_to_grid"
    },
    {
      "kind": "street_orientation",
      "id": "claim-34ave-parallel",
      "text": "34th Avenue runs parallel to the neighborhood's east-west numbered avenues, forming the spine of the garden-apartment blocks.",
      "street": "34 AVE",
      "relation": "parallel_to_grid"
    },
    {
      "kind": "street_orientation",
      "id": "claim-37ave-parallel",
      "text": "37th Avenue runs parallel to the grid's avenues, anchoring the commercial corridor that serves the residential blocks around it.",
      "street": "37 AVE",
      "relation": "parallel_to_grid"
    },
    {
      "kind": "street_orientation",
      "id": "claim-82st-parallel",
      "text": "82nd Street runs parallel to the grid's numbered streets, framing the western edge of the historic district's garden blocks.",
      "street": "82 ST",
      "relation": "parallel_to_grid"
    },
    {
      "kind": "street_orientation",
      "id": "claim-junction-diagonal",
      "text": "Junction Boulevard crosses the grid diagonally, marking where older road alignments converge with the planned street system.",
      "street": "JUNCTION BLVD",
      "relation": "diagonal_to_grid"
    },
    {
      "kind": "layer_stat",
      "id": "claim-segment-count",
      "text": "The neighborhood's street network is built from 1,001 mapped street segments, a fine-grained grid that gave each block consistent proportions for the garden courts set inside them.",
      "layer": "streets_centerline",
      "field": null,
      "agg": "count",
      "expected": 1001,
      "tolerance": 0
    },
    {
      "kind": "layer_stat",
      "id": "claim-width-mean",
      "text": "Street widths average 32.6 feet across the neighborhood, a modest scale that let planners devote more land to interior gardens than to roadway.",
      "layer": "streets_centerline",
      "field": "st_width",
      "agg": "mean",
      "expected": 32.6,
      "tolerance": 0.1
    },
    {
      "kind": "layer_stat",
      "id": "claim-width-max",
      "text": "The widest street segments reach 70 feet, reserved for the boulevards like Northern and Roosevelt that carry regional traffic through the area.",
      "layer": "streets_centerline",
      "field": "st_width",
      "agg": "max",
      "expected": 70,
      "tolerance": 0
    },
    {
      "kind": "layer_stat",
      "id": "claim-width-min",
      "text": "The narrowest street segments measure just 6 feet, likely service lanes or mews tucked between the garden-apartment courts.",
      "layer": "streets_centerline",
      "field": "st_width",
      "agg": "min",
      "expected": 6,
      "tolerance": 0
    }
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "A Grid Interrupted",
      "intro": "Jackson Heights sits on a neighborhood-scale street grid of numbered avenues and streets, but that grid was never a blank slate. Two older roads, Broadway and Northern Boulevard, cut across it at an angle, remnants of a colonial-era road network that predates the twentieth-century planning that created the neighborhood we know today.",
      "cites": ["nyc-lpc", "nyc-opendata"],
      "claim_ids": ["claim-northern-diagonal", "claim-broadway-diagonal"],
      "slides": [
        {
          "title": "The Legacy Diagonals",
          "body": "Northern Boulevard and Broadway both slice across the numbered grid at an angle. These were established travel routes long before developers laid out Jackson Heights' orderly avenues, and their diagonal paths still define irregular, wedge-shaped blocks where they meet the grid.",
          "map": {"item_id": "81186482d5dd48ca9882593adadd6bfe", "caption": "Northern Boulevard and Broadway crossing the numbered grid at an angle."},
          "claim_ids": ["claim-northern-diagonal", "claim-broadway-diagonal"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Junction Boulevard's Crossing",
          "body": "Junction Boulevard is another diagonal thread, marking a point where older alignments converge with the planned grid. Its name itself hints at this role: a junction between street systems built in different eras.",
          "map": {"item_id": "81186482d5dd48ca9882593adadd6bfe", "caption": "Junction Boulevard crossing the grid diagonally near the neighborhood's edge."},
          "claim_ids": ["claim-junction-diagonal"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Building the Garden Blocks",
      "intro": "Inside the grid's rectilinear blocks, developers in the 1910s and 1920s built garden apartments organized around the parallel avenues that give the neighborhood its rhythm. Streets like 34th Avenue, 37th Avenue, and 82nd Street run true to the grid, and their consistent spacing let architects repeat a formula: buildings wrapped around private, shared gardens.",
      "cites": ["nyc-lpc", "city-lore"],
      "claim_ids": ["claim-34ave-parallel", "claim-37ave-parallel", "claim-82st-parallel"],
      "slides": [
        {
          "title": "34th Avenue: The Garden Spine",
          "body": "34th Avenue runs parallel to the grid's other avenues, and the blocks along it hold some of the neighborhood's best-known garden apartment complexes. The street's regularity let each complex align its internal courtyards with its neighbors.",
          "map": {"item_id": "865dbb45a3b3455590652560174b9ed9", "caption": "34th Avenue and the garden apartment blocks aligned along it."},
          "claim_ids": ["claim-34ave-parallel"]
        },
        {
          "title": "37th Avenue: Where Commerce Meets the Grid",
          "body": "37th Avenue, also parallel to the grid, became the neighborhood's commercial spine, lined with shops and the elevated train that brought residents to and from Manhattan. It shows how the same grid logic could support both quiet garden blocks and a busy retail corridor.",
          "map": {"item_id": "865dbb45a3b3455590652560174b9ed9", "caption": "37th Avenue's commercial corridor running parallel to the numbered grid."},
          "claim_ids": ["claim-37ave-parallel"]
        },
        {
          "title": "82nd Street: The District's Edge",
          "body": "82nd Street frames the western boundary of the historic district's garden blocks, running parallel to the grid's numbered streets and helping define where the planned, garden-centered neighborhood gives way to the surrounding city.",
          "map": {"item_id": "865dbb45a3b3455590652560174b9ed9", "caption": "82nd Street marking the historic district's western edge."},
          "claim_ids": ["claim-82st-parallel"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "A Network Measured in Feet",
      "intro": "The scale of Jackson Heights' streets, not just their layout, shaped how much room was left for open space. Across 1,001 mapped segments, most streets stay modest in width, leaving land inside the blocks for the shared gardens that define the neighborhood's character.",
      "cites": ["nyc-opendata", "nyc-parks"],
      "claim_ids": ["claim-segment-count", "claim-width-mean"],
      "slides": [
        {
          "title": "A Thousand Segments, One System",
          "body": "The street network is stitched together from 1,001 individual segments. That granularity, block by block, let planners vary street widths precisely: wider on boulevards carrying traffic, narrower on residential blocks cradling garden courts.",
          "map": {"item_id": "81186482d5dd48ca9882593adadd6bfe", "caption": "The full network of 1,001 street segments across Jackson Heights."},
          "claim_ids": ["claim-segment-count"]
        },
        {
          "title": "From 6 Feet to 70",
          "body": "Street widths range from just 6 feet on the narrowest service lanes to 70 feet on boulevards like Northern and Roosevelt, with the typical street averaging about 32.6 feet. That modest average meant more of each block's footprint could be given to the gardens at its center rather than to pavement.",
          "map": {"item_id": "865dbb45a3b3455590652560174b9ed9", "caption": "Street widths across the neighborhood, from narrow mews to major boulevards."},
          "claim_ids": ["claim-width-mean", "claim-width-max", "claim-width-min"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Streets in Service of Gardens",
      "intro": "The relationship ran both ways: the grid's regularity enabled the garden apartment movement, and the gardens in turn made the grid livable, turning what could have been monotonous blocks into a celebrated planned community. Today, NYC Parks-mapped open spaces and the Landmarks-designated historic district together trace this mutual shaping of circulation and greenery.",
      "cites": ["nyc-parks", "nyc-lpc", "city-lore"],
      "claim_ids": [],
      "slides": [
        {
          "title": "A Model Still Standing",
          "body": "Jackson Heights' designated historic district preserves this interplay: a grid interrupted by legacy diagonals, organized by parallel residential avenues, and built at a street scale that prioritized shared green space over roadway. The result is a neighborhood where the street system and the open space system remain legible as two halves of one plan.",
          "map": {"item_id": "865dbb45a3b3455590652560174b9ed9", "caption": "The historic district today, where streets and gardens still define each other."},
          "claim_ids": []
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

