 the grid, one of the regularly spaced north-south numbered streets.","street":"82 ST","relation":"parallel_to_grid"},{"kind":"layer_stat","id":"c8","text":"The street centerline data records 1,001 mapped street segments across the study area.","layer":"streets_centerline","field":null,"agg":"count","expected":1001,"tolerance":0},{"kind":"layer_stat","id":"c9","text":"Street segments average 32.6 feet in width, a modest, human-scaled dimension typical of a residential grid.","layer":"streets_centerline","field":"st_width","agg":"mean","expected":32.6,"tolerance":0.1},{"kind":"layer_stat","id":"c10","text":"The narrowest mapped street segment is just 6 feet wide, indicating service lanes and courtyard-access paths threaded between buildings.","layer":"streets_centerline","field":"st_width","agg":"min","expected":6,"tolerance":0},{"kind":"layer_stat","id":"c11","text":"The widest mapped street segment reaches 70 feet, matching the grand boulevards that frame the neighborhood's open spaces.","layer":"streets_centerline","field":"st_width","agg":"max","expected":70,"tolerance":0}],"sections":[{"title":"A Grid Imposed on Farmland","intro":"In the early 1900s, the Queensboro Corporation overlaid a strict rectilinear grid of numbered streets and avenues onto former farmland, creating the orderly blocks that would later hold the neighborhood's garden apartments.","cites":["nyc-lpc","city-lore"],"claim_ids":["c8","c5","c7"],"slides":[{"title":"One Thousand Segments of Order","body":"The street centerline data captures 1,001 segments, a dense mesh of blocks that let planners parcel the farmland into buildable units.","map":{"item_id":"81186482d5dd48ca9882593adadd6bfe","caption":"The full street network of Jackson Heights, Hard System Layers web map."},"claim_ids":["c8"]},{"title":"Avenues That Hold the Line","body":"34th Avenue runs parallel to the grid, anchoring rows of garden-apartment courts along its length.","map":{"item_id":"865dbb45a3b3455590652560174b9ed9","caption":"34th Avenue within the grid, Street & Open Space Profile web map."},"claim_ids":["c5"]},{"title":"Streets Running True","body":"82nd Street runs precisely parallel to its neighboring numbered streets, giving every block the same proportions.","map":{"item_id":"865dbb45a3b3455590652560174b9ed9","caption":"82nd Street in the regular numbered-street grid."},"claim_ids":["c7"]}]},{"title":"Broadway's Lone Diagonal","intro":"Only Broadway truly cuts against the grid; the neighborhood's other old roads and the elevated transit line, despite looking like relics of an earlier landscape, actually run close to the grid's own axis.","cites":["nyc-lpc","city-lore"],"claim_ids":["c1","c2","c3","c4"],"slides":[{"title":"Broadway's Stubborn Angle","body":"Broadway slices diagonally across the neighborhood, a surviving pre-grid route planners had to design around.","map":{"item_id":"81186482d5dd48ca9882593adadd6bfe","caption":"Broadway crossing the grid at an angle."},"claim_ids":["c1"]},{"title":"Northern Boulevard's Quiet Alignment","body":"Northern Boulevard, once a diagonal country road, now deviates only slightly from the grid's axis, running nearly parallel.","map":{"item_id":"81186482d5dd48ca9882593adadd6bfe","caption":"Northern Boulevard's route alongside the grid."},"claim_ids":["c2"]},{"title":"Roosevelt Avenue Keeps Step","body":"Roosevelt Avenue, beneath the elevated IRT line, aligns closely with the grid despite its older, pre-development origins.","map":{"item_id":"865dbb45a3b3455590652560174b9ed9","caption":"Roosevelt Avenue and the elevated transit corridor."},"claim_ids":["c3"]},{"title":"Junction Boulevard's Near Match","body":"Junction Boulevard's angle nearly matches the grid axis, a near-seamless stitch between two historically separate street systems.","map":{"item_id":"81186482d5dd48ca9882593adadd6bfe","caption":"Junction Boulevard near the grid's edge."},"claim_ids":["c4"]}]},{"title":"Streets Sized for a Garden City","intro":"Street width in Jackson Heights was never uniform: narrow service lanes threaded past interior gardens while wide avenues framed the neighborhood's open spaces and commercial life.","cites":["nyc-opendata","nyc-parks"],"claim_ids":["c9","c10","c11","c6"],"slides":[{"title":"A Modest Average Width","body":"Across 1,001 segments, streets average 32.6 feet wide, narrow enough to keep traffic calm and pedestrian-scaled.","map":{"item_id":"865dbb45a3b3455590652560174b9ed9","caption":"Street widths across the neighborhood, symbolized by the st_width field."},"claim_ids":["c9"]},{"title":"The Narrowest Threads","body":"At its narrowest, a mapped segment measures only 6 feet, a service lane threaded between apartment blocks.","map":{"item_id":"865dbb45a3b3455590652560174b9ed9","caption":"A narrow service lane adjacent to interior courtyards."},"claim_ids":["c10"]},{"title":"The Widest Boulevards","body":"At the other extreme, a segment reaches 70 feet, the width of grand boulevards framing open space.","map":{"item_id":"81186482d5dd48ca9882593adadd6bfe","caption":"A wide boulevard segment framing open space."},"claim_ids":["c11"]},{"title":"37th Avenue's Commercial Spine","body":"37th Avenue runs parallel to the grid, forming the commercial spine between rows of garden-apartment courtyards.","map":{"item_id":"865dbb45a3b3455590652560174b9ed9","caption":"37th Avenue's commercial corridor within the grid."},"claim_ids":["c6"]}]}]}
